Copyright (c) 2026 Jordan Gibbs.

Why not MIT like the rest of Ochre: the model was trained with openWakeWord / livekit-wakeword
data (precomputed ACAV100M features, freesound background noise, MIT room impulse responses),
some of which may only be used non-commercially. openWakeWord licenses its own pre-trained
models CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 for the same reason, and this model follows it.

What this means:
- Ochre's source code, including the training pipeline in training/wakeword, stays MIT.
- You may use, share and adapt this model for non-commercial purposes, with attribution, and
  share adaptations under the same licence.
- Commercial use of the hands-free wake word needs a model trained on data that allows it.
  training/wakeword can retrain one from your own negatives; the app loads whatever model
  `handsfree.model` points to. Everything else in Ochre works without this file.
